from . import T
T('3-c-memory','c-expressions','Programming in C;Expressions','C types, conversions and expression evaluation',r'''
C expressions are evaluated according to operand types, operator rules and sequencing guarantees. Integer division truncates toward zero; an operation using a floating operand normally performs a floating conversion before arithmetic. Assigning an already computed integer quotient to double converts the truncated result and cannot recover its discarded fraction. Operator precedence determines grouping, not necessarily temporal evaluation order.
Some expressions have undefined behavior, such as accessing beyond an array, signed integer overflow, or certain unsequenced modifications of one object. Undefined behavior does not have one deterministic answer to “trace” across compilers. Implementation-defined behavior instead requires a documented platform choice; unspecified behavior permits several allowed choices. Character and integer widths, signedness and pointer sizes should follow the supplied machine model.
Short-circuit && and || sequence their left operand before a conditionally evaluated right operand. Bitwise & and | do not provide that same control-flow behavior. Parenthesize a complex expression to determine its parse, then analyze conversions and sequencing separately. GATE code problems can test whether a proposed output is even defined, not merely whether arithmetic is correct.
''','Compare double a=7/2; and double b=7/2.0;.',r'Integer operation::7/2=3::Both operands are int, so fractional remainder is discarded.~Assignment conversion::a=3.0::Conversion happens after that division.~Floating operation::7/2.0=3.5::The floating operand changes the arithmetic before division.', 'a=3.0 and b=3.5.','The destination type alone does not determine the earlier subexpression’s arithmetic type.')
T('3-c-memory','pointers','Pointers;Arrays','Arrays, pointer arithmetic and bounds',r'''
An array stores a fixed sequence of contiguous elements of one type. Its index-zero address is the base, and index i lies i times the element width beyond it. In most expressions an array converts to a pointer to its first element, but sizeof on an actual array object obtains its entire storage extent rather than pointer size. An array-form function parameter is adjusted to a pointer parameter.
A pointer stores an address with a pointed-to type. Adding j advances by j elements, not j raw bytes; subtracting pointers within one array gives an element difference. A one-past pointer may be formed and compared under the language rules but cannot be dereferenced. Access outside the permitted object range is not repaired by memory happening to exist nearby.
Distinguish a pointer variable’s storage from the pointed-to object. Passing a pointer copies the address value, permitting modification of that object, but reassigning the parameter alone does not change the caller’s pointer variable. Track lifetime: pointers to automatic locals become invalid when those objects cease to exist. Array-to-pointer conversion does not imply arrays and pointer variables are identical types.
''','For int a[5] and sizeof(int)=4, p=&a[1]. Where does p+2 point?',r'Locate current index::::p refers to element one, not the beginning of the array.~Apply element scaling::byte\ displacement=2\cdot4=8::Two int-sized steps are eight bytes.~Determine target::p+2=\&a[3]::The index advances from one to three within bounds.', 'a[3], eight bytes beyond p.','Subtracting (p+2)−p yields 2 elements, not 8 bytes.')
T('3-c-memory','storage','Storage','Scope, lifetime and storage duration in C',r'''
Scope tells where a name is visible; lifetime tells when its object exists. Automatic locals typically exist during a block activation, with a distinct instance in each recursive call. Static-duration objects exist for the program’s execution; a static local keeps its value across calls despite its name having local scope. Dynamically allocated objects persist until released, independent of the block containing the pointer variable.
Uninitialized automatic scalar values are indeterminate, while static-duration objects without explicit initializers are initialized to zero. Allocation failure must be checked before dereferencing a returned pointer. A freed object cannot be validly read just because its former bytes have not visibly changed; double-free and use-after-free are invalid operations.
For tracing, maintain separate objects and name bindings. A local variable shadowing a global accesses a different object unless the code explicitly obtains the global through another means. “Global,” “heap” and “stack” are implementation concepts related to these rules but not a substitute for the language lifetime specification. Function-return questions commonly expose pointers to dead automatic storage rather than a legitimate returned array.
''','A function uses static int x=0; then increments and returns x on each call.',r'Initialization::::x is initialized once to zero, not once per call.~First call::x=0+1=1::The object persists after return.~Second call::x=1+1=2::The next call sees the previously stored value.', 'The first two returns are 1 and 2.','Replacing static with an initialized automatic local would instead restart at zero on every call.')
T('3-recursion','calls','Recursion;Call stack','Recursion, base cases and suspended calls',r'''
A recursive function calls itself on smaller or otherwise progressing instances. A base case ends the descent, and a progress measure must guarantee that calls eventually reach it for the permitted input. A recursive call does not replace all state of the caller: each active call retains parameters, locals and a return position until its child returns.
To trace output, separate statements before the recursive call from those after it. Before-call prints occur while descending; after-call prints occur while unwinding. Draw an activation stack and resume each frame after its call statement. Multiple recursive branches execute according to the language’s sequencing, and total calls can greatly exceed maximum simultaneous depth.
A return value flows into the suspended caller’s expression; a print statement and a returned value are different observations. Tail recursion has no remaining work after the recursive call, but C does not generally guarantee elimination of its stack frame. Do not assume constant stack just because a source function is tail-recursive; follow the problem’s implementation model.
''','Trace f(n): if n=0 return; print n; f(n−1); print n, for n=2.',r'Descend::::f(2) prints 2, and f(1) prints 1 before calling f(0).~Base case::::f(0) returns without printing, restoring the suspended f(1).~Unwind::::f(1) prints 1; then f(2) prints 2.', 'Output sequence 2,1,1,2.','There are two nonbase calls and each prints twice; four values must appear.')
T('3-recursion','cost','Recursive complexity','Call count, recursion depth and auxiliary space',r'''
Recursive complexity counts total work across the whole call tree, while stack space counts the largest simultaneously active path. For one child per call with decreasing n and constant local work, the call tree is a chain and time and depth are linear. For two child calls, the tree can grow exponentially even if each root-to-leaf path has only linear depth.
Write a recurrence from the actual child sizes and nonrecursive work. Count base calls too when the question asks invocations. Memory per frame matters: a recursion of logarithmic depth can still use more than logarithmic space if each frame stores a large array. Sequential child branches typically do not coexist as complete subtrees in memory.
For naive Fibonacci, repeated subproblems cause exponential calls; memoization changes this by storing results. For factorial, a chain down to zero contains n+1 calls but n multiplications. Asymptotic notation and exact counts answer different questions. State whether memory includes the input/output storage and whether the implementation performs tail-call optimization before giving an auxiliary-space bound.
''','Factorial(n) calls factorial(n−1), with base factorial(0). Find calls and stack depth at n=4.',r'Chain::::The active values descend 4,3,2,1,0.~Count invocations::N_{calls}=4+1=5::Include the base call.~Maximum depth::D=5::All five calls can be simultaneously active immediately before the base returns.', 'Five invocations and maximum depth five, assuming one frame per call.','Only four multiplications occur; operations and function-call count are not interchangeable.')
T('3-linear-structures','lists','Linked lists','Linked lists and operation costs',r'''
A linked list stores nodes connected by pointers rather than one contiguous indexed array. A singly linked node points forward; a doubly linked node also points backward. Head references the beginning, and a null link convention marks the end unless the structure is circular. Nodes can be individually allocated and need not have neighboring addresses.
Insertion after a known node is constant time by updating a few pointers; locating the kth node takes traversal. Deleting a singly linked node generally needs its predecessor to repair the incoming link. Head and empty-list cases need separate updates. In a doubly linked list, neighbor pointers enable constant-time deletion when the node is known, but both directions must be repaired.
Big-O claims must state what is already given. “Delete a key” includes search unless a node reference is supplied. Compared with arrays, lists allow local structural updates without shifting a suffix but pay pointer overhead and weaker locality. A circular list needs a stopping condition other than null to avoid an infinite traversal. Preserve references before changing links, or an allocated suffix can become unreachable.
''','Insert X between singly linked nodes A→B.',r'Preserve successor::::Save or retain B, currently A.next.~Attach new node::::Set X.next=B before overwriting the existing incoming link.~Redirect predecessor::::Set A.next=X, giving A→X→B.', 'The new chain is A→X→B, with constant pointer-update work when A is known.','If A.next is changed first and B is not retained, the original suffix can be lost.')
T('3-linear-structures','stacks','Stacks','Stacks, postfix evaluation and operand order',r'''
A stack is last-in-first-out. Push adds to the top, pop removes the top, and peek reads it without removal. An array implementation tracks a top index and capacity; a linked implementation uses a head pointer. Underflow means removing from an empty stack; overflow depends on the representation’s capacity or allocation limits.
Stacks support nested operations such as balanced-parenthesis checking, recursion and postfix expression evaluation. For postfix, push operands; on a binary operator pop right operand first, then left, compute left operator right and push the result. The order is essential for subtraction and division. A valid complete expression leaves exactly one value, and no operator can require more operands than available.
Balanced-parenthesis algorithms push open symbols and match each closing symbol against the top, detecting incorrect nesting as well as unmatched count. Equal numbers of open and close symbols alone do not establish correctness. Complexity is linear in input length when each symbol is pushed/popped at most once, with stack space proportional to maximum nesting or pending operands.
''','Evaluate postfix 8 3 − 2 *.',r'First operator::8-3=5::Pop 3 as right operand and 8 as left.~Second operator::5\cdot2=10::The earlier result is the next operator’s left operand.~Validate stack::::The final stack has one value and no operation underflowed.', '10.','The corresponding infix expression is (8−3)×2. Reversing pop order at subtraction gives a wrong negative result.')
T('3-linear-structures','queues','Queues;Circular queue','FIFO queues and circular-buffer conventions',r'''
A queue is first-in-first-out: enqueue adds at the rear and dequeue removes at the front. An array queue can waste released prefix space if indices only move forward; a circular queue wraps them modulo capacity. A linked queue usually retains both head and tail for constant-time endpoint operations.
Full/empty representation is a design choice. If head and tail coincide when empty, using the same condition for full is ambiguous unless a count or separate full flag exists. A common alternative reserves one slot, making usable capacity N−1; that is not a universal property of every circular queue. A stored count permits all N slots and distinguishes the cases.
Trace whether tail denotes last occupied or next free; update formulas differ between conventions. Enqueue requires available capacity and dequeue requires nonemptiness before moving indices. Circular indexing changes placement, not FIFO order. When calculating complexity, exclude resizing or include its amortized effect if the implementation grows; a fixed-capacity queue has straightforward constant-time successful endpoint operations.
''','Capacity 5 with a count variable; head=3, tail=0, count=2. Dequeue then enqueue X.',r'Dequeue::::Remove the element at slot 3; head becomes 4 and count becomes 1.~Enqueue::::Store X in next-free slot 0; tail becomes 1 and count becomes 2.~Interpret::::The older remaining element is still removed before X, regardless of wrapped positions.', 'Final head 4, tail 1, count 2.','Indices alone may look numerically out of order, but modulo placement preserves logical FIFO order.')
T('3-trees-bst','trees','Trees;Tree traversals','Tree terminology and traversal order',r'''
A rooted tree has a distinguished root; each other node has one parent and can have children. Depth counts edges from the root, and height counts the longest downward edge path under the edge convention. Leaves have no children. A binary tree allows at most two children but is not automatically ordered by keys.
Preorder visits node then left then right; inorder visits left then node then right; postorder visits left then right then node. Level order uses a queue. These rules are recursive visit schedules, not arbitrary visual scanning. A perfect binary tree fills every level; a complete tree fills levels except possibly the last, whose nodes occupy leftmost positions. These definitions are not synonyms.
Traversal reconstruction questions need assumptions such as distinct keys. Inorder plus preorder or postorder can reconstruct a binary tree with distinct labels; preorder plus postorder alone generally cannot uniquely determine it. A BST’s inorder sequence is sorted because of its key invariant, not because all binary trees have sorted inorder. Count nodes and edges separately when converting height formulas.
''','A tree has root A, left child B with left child D, and right child C.',r'Preorder::::Visit root first: A,B,D,C.~Inorder::::Finish each left subtree before its root: D,B,A,C.~Postorder::::Finish children before root: D,B,C,A.', 'Preorder A B D C; inorder D B A C; postorder D B C A.','Every traversal visits each node exactly once; only the visit moment changes.')
T('3-trees-bst','bst','Binary search trees','BST search, insertion and deletion',r'''
A binary search tree stores smaller keys in a node’s left subtree and larger keys in its right, under a specified duplicate policy. Search compares at each node and follows one branch, giving O(h) time for height h. Insertion follows the same comparisons to an empty position. A balanced shape makes h logarithmic, but sorted insertions can create a linear-height chain.
Deletion has three cases: remove a leaf, replace a one-child node by that child, or replace a two-child node’s key by its inorder successor/predecessor and delete that replacement’s old node. The successor is the minimum of the right subtree and may itself have a right child. It is not necessarily the root’s immediate right child.
After every operation verify the invariant for whole subtrees, not just parent-child comparisons. Rotations used by balanced variants preserve inorder order but change shape. A plain BST does not rebalance automatically. GATE traces may depend on successor versus predecessor replacement and duplicate conventions, so use the stated policy instead of assuming one universal deletion result.
''','Delete 5 from BST with root 5, left 2, right 8, and 8’s left child 6.',r'Find successor::::The smallest right-subtree key is 6, not 8.~Replace root key::::Store 6 at the root while retaining both subtrees.~Remove old successor::::Delete the original leaf 6 from beneath 8.', 'Root becomes 6, with left 2 and right 8.','Inorder is 2,6,8, so all remaining keys still satisfy the BST ordering.')
T('3-heaps-graphs','heaps','Binary heaps','Binary heaps and why build-heap is linear',r'''
A binary heap is a complete binary tree with a local ordering property: each parent is at most its children in a min-heap, or at least them in a max-heap. It is not a BST and siblings/subtrees are not globally sorted. Zero-based array children are 2i+1 and 2i+2, with parent floor((i−1)/2).
Insertion appends at the next complete position and bubbles upward. Removing the root moves the last element to the root, shrinks the array and sifts downward toward the appropriate child. These operations take O(log n) because height is logarithmic. Finding an arbitrary key can still take O(n).
Bottom-up build-heap sifts internal nodes from the last one toward the root. Its total cost is O(n), not n log n: many nodes sit close to leaves and have little downward work, while very few have large height. Summing nodes at height h times O(h) yields a convergent weighted series times n. Repeated insertion is a different construction and has a weaker worst-case O(n log n) bound.
''','Insert 1 into min-heap [2,5,3].',r'Append::::Array becomes [2,5,3,1], preserving completeness.~First repair::::Compare index 3 to parent 1 and swap: [2,1,3,5].~Second repair::::Compare index 1 to parent 0 and swap: [1,2,3,5].', 'Heap [1,2,3,5].','Every parent is at most its children; globally sorted order happened here but is not required for heaps.')
T('3-heaps-graphs','representations','Adjacency list;Adjacency matrix','Graph representations and operation costs',r'''
An adjacency matrix reserves a V×V entry for each possible ordered vertex pair, indicating edge presence or storing weight. It takes Θ(V²) space and offers constant-time membership tests. Enumerating one vertex’s neighbors scans V entries even if its degree is tiny. A weighted “no edge” sentinel must not confuse a valid zero-weight edge with absence.
An adjacency list stores each vertex’s actual neighbors. Space is Θ(V+E), with undirected edges normally appearing in both endpoint lists. Enumerating neighbors costs proportional to degree; testing a particular edge can require scanning a list unless an additional search structure is used. Lists are attractive for sparse graphs while matrices can be convenient for dense graphs.
Representation affects traversal complexity, not just storage. BFS/DFS using lists scans all lists in Θ(V+E); matrix traversal typically scans Θ(V²). Parallel edges may need repeated list entries or richer matrix cells. Directed graphs need not have symmetric matrices. Before a complexity MCQ, identify representation, graph direction and the operation required instead of memorizing one bound for “graphs.”
''','Compare V=1000, E=2000 directed edges.',r'Matrix entries::V^2=10^6::Most allocated positions may be absent edges.~List entries::::Store 2000 neighbor entries plus 1000 vertex headers, with implementation-dependent overhead.~Operation tradeoff::::The matrix tests an edge directly; a list enumerates only actual neighbors.', 'Lists offer much smaller asymptotic storage for this sparse graph.','For an undirected interpretation, edge entries would double to 4000 but remain Θ(V+E).')
